00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.750 align:start position:0%
 
hey 42 here  it was a normal day on the planet  alderaan when as if from nowhere a  moonlike blob appeared in the sky  not that many of the planet's two  billion inhabitants actually noticed  the unidentified object was tiny  so only people who happened to be  looking in exactly the right spot when  it appeared  would have had any chance of seeing it  to those lucky few i can only assume the  sudden appearance of a brand new mini  moon in the sky was quite an exciting  even awe-inspiring event  but that excitement didn't last very  long  because all of a sudden the object began  to glow  seconds later alderaan exploded with a  blinding green light that seemed to  devour the entire sky as a blast of pure  heat vaporized the air and boils the  oceans  in a matter of seconds  it was over  the beautiful mountain planet of  alderaan was gone  and everyone who'd been unfortunate  enough to live there  at the time  was dead  okay so for all you non-star wars geeks  out there what i just described was the  laser-based destruction of the planet  alderaan by the empire's iconic doomsday  device the death star  it's a seminal scene in the first star  wars film and arguably one of the most  famous moments in all of cinema  and it got me thinking is a  planet-killing weapon like the death  star actually a physical possibility and  if it is  could i build one  i mean could we that is humans build one  sorry always wanted to be a super  villain when i was growing up  [Music]  speaking of being an evil genius i'm a  huge fan of stellaris a grand strategy  space exploration game where you can  really become the evil intergalactic  dictator you've always dreamt of  becoming or if you'd prefer you can  become a nature loving species of  anthropoids oh and did i mention yes you  can destroy a planet in stellaris  nice  actually stellaris offers multiple ways  to destroy a planet you can research and  build a colossal ship there are three  classes of colossal ship the colossus  the juggernaut and the star eater each  one can destroy a planet after you equip  it with the celestial body eradicating  weapon of your choosing would you prefer  to use a global pacifier to destroy a  planet  or the world cracker  my money's on the latter furthermore if  you build the star eater colossus you  can equip it with the star cracker  weapon and devour entire stars you can  have a go at destroying planets for free  this weekend by trying stellaris using  the link in the description don't miss  out thanks to stellaris for sponsoring  this video  the first thing i want to point out here  and forgive me for stating the obvious  is that destroying a planet of roughly  the size and composition of earth is  quite a hard thing to do  you may have heard a rumor that if we  were to detonate all the nuclear weapons  on earth at the same time the planet  would explode but that couldn't be  further from the truth  on a planetary scale we humans and our  grand schemes  are practically irrelevant you'll often  hear people talking about how we're  killing the planet through things like  climate change and mass deforestation  but we really aren't like not even a  tiny bit  what we're actually doing is making our  planet significantly less livable for  ourselves and every other living thing  that's found here as for the planet  itself  seriously it couldn't give any less of a  earth is a six septillion kilogram  wrecking ball made of rock and iron  six  septillion  that's a six with 24 zeros after it in  the past 600 million years alone it  survived impacts from at least 60  asteroids with a diameter of five or  more kilometers one of the largest of  those struck earth 66 million years ago  and according to some estimates it might  have been close to 100 kilometers across  it's smashed into our planet with the  explosive power of 10 billion nuclear  weapons wiping out 75 of life including  all non-avian dinosaurs and leaving  behind a 180 kilometer wide crater on a  human scale it was an unimaginably  devastating event  a genuine apocalypse but the planet as a  whole hardly even noticed  what i'm trying to say is that  in technical terms planet earth is one  chunky boy  destroying it or a planet like it would  require a weapon trillions of times more  powerful than anything in existence  today as for what that weapon might look  like well it turns out something like  the death star might actually be a  pretty good place to start when i say  the word laser there's a fair chance  your mind clunges up images if those  little laser pointers fancy pens  business types like to use in  presentations  but even today military-grade laser  technology is already pretty frightening  stuff with the power to target and  destroy aircraft and even satellites  mid-flight so all we'd need to do to  make a weapon of death star proportions  is scale things up a bit well i say a  bit to completely destroy something as  big as a planet we need to develop a  laser with around a billion trillion  times more power than the lasers we have  today and even if we were actually able  to do it  such great power would inevitably come  with great economic responsibilities  back in 2012 some bright sparks set up a  petition to the us government in an  attempt to secure funding for the  construction of an actual death star  citing the very reasonable benefits of a  boost to national security and the  creation of millions of jobs  the petition gained almost 35 000  signatures and remarkably enough the  white house actually responded  sadly that response was a hard no due to  an estimated total build cost of about  850 quadrillion dollars  about 13 000 times the gdp of every  country on earth put together at the  time  there would also be plenty of  engineering problems to overcome too  no lazing material known to man would be  able to withstand the incredible heat of  such a device  and whatever humanity's death star  itself was actually made out of would  melt like an easter egg in a blast  furnace as soon as it was turned on  hey maybe one day it'll be possible and  like in stellaris we'll see world  cracking glazes but realistically we're  talking hundreds or more likely  thousands of years in humanity's future  luckily there's more than one way to  skin a cat  and indeed destroy a planet  and at least one of those ways employs a  technology or should i say substance  that we've already been producing  for a good 70 years or so  antimatter  think of antimatter as being a bit like  regular mata's evil twin a material  composed of anti-particles with  properties that are the exact opposite  of regular matter  antimatter is fascinating stuff that  deserves an entire video on its own but  for the purposes of this one all we need  to know is that when antimatter and  regular mata meet they annihilate each  other releasing vast amounts of energy  so much energy that a single gram of  antimatter could create an explosion  comparable in size to that of a nuclear  bomb  the very fact that it instantly blows up  when it comes into contact with regular  matter which as you probably noticed is  absolutely bloody everywhere  makes antimatter rather difficult to  handle  it can't be stored in any kind of  conventional  based containers for obvious reasons  instead needing to be held suspended  within powerful magnetic fields and it  turns out that isn't even the biggest  issue we'd have to overcome to build a  viable anti-matter bomb as we've already  established earth is a chunky boy and so  we'd still need a few trillion tons of  antimatter to destroy it  but making antimatter is tricky  to do so you need amongst other things a  powerful particle accelerator like the  large hadron collider at cern in  switzerland but even if you were to run  that formidable machine non-stop as a  dedicated anti-matter factory  it would still only produce about a  billionth of a gram of antimatter  per year  meaning the entire lifespan of the  universe  might not provide us with enough time to  make an antimatter based doomsday device  capable of chunking a planet even if it  did the total cost of the project would  make building a death star  look like a bargain  estimates vary but as of today creating  a gram of antimatter costs about 63  trillion dollars  just as was the case with lasers the  problem here is one of scale  one shutting something as big as a  planet requires us to push the kind of  destructive technologies we currently  know about to almost unimaginable limits  but then  maybe we've been looking at this problem  from the wrong perspective  what if instead of building one giant  ending weapon  we just make a crap ton of little ones  well not weapons exactly more like  weaponized self-replicating robots  it's an idea that's being explored by  such scientific heavyweights as freeman  dyson and john von neumann and it's both  incredibly simple and absolutely  ingenious  imagine you designed a robot of roughly  the same combination of materials that  your target planet is made out of  now imagine that robot had the  capability both to harvest those  materials  and use them to create an exact copy of  itself theory all you'd have to do is  build and activate a single one of those  robots to destroy an entire planet let  it loose and as per its programming the  robot would head out into the world  looking for a likely spot  then start to dig once it had collected  all the raw materials necessary it would  pause to build a clone of itself  once complete both the clone and its  parent would move on harvesting more raw  materials and turning them into new  generations of clones intuitively you  might assume it would take forever to  destroy a planet this way but the number  of clones would double with every  generation  meaning the total population will grow  exponentially after just 40 generations  more than a trillion self-replicating  robots would be busy taking your planet  apart piece by piece  all this talk of self-replicating robots  reminds me of the grey tempest special  event in stellaris i fondly remember the  first time i encountered it in game  after passing through a mysterious warp  gate i discovered in a far-off star  system it really freaked me out and  before i knew it my homeworld was being  bombarded by alien nanites slowly  devouring my population it might sound  like a fairly outlandish concept  but think about it  what i've just described an autonomous  machine that harvests raw materials from  its environment in order to make copies  of itself isn't that just an accurate  description  of a human being or any other animal for  that matter there would still be some  fairly major challenges to overcome  along the way like exactly how these  machines would handle temperatures of  more than 5000 degrees celsius in the  planet's core for example but in many  ways self-replicating robots might be  the most feasible way for humans to  destroy a planet  partly because they completely  circumvent the whole scale issue we see  with more conventional weapons  with all this talk of how we could  harness human technology to build the  most destructive weapons in existence  it's easy to forget that there are  already untold trillions of super  weapons  basically just lying around all over the  place throughout the entire universe we  don't tend to think of them that way of  course but there's a good chance nothing  human-made will ever rival the sheer  might and majesty of your average  run-of-the-mill star or world-ending  terror of a bog-standard black hole  so rather than spending our time  dreaming up inventive ways to speed up  anti-matter production or dynamics our  lasers  we might be better off just shooting  whatever it is we want to destroy  at the nearest star or black hole  either of which will be capable of  swallowing a planet the size of earth as  easily as you might eat and after dinner  mint  moving something as big as a planet is  of course a mammoth task that presents a  fair few scale problems all of its own  but for now it might prove to be rather  easier than blowing one up entirely  thanks for watching

